\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/09-nbe-hoas-tricks.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Scope: what the two \code{\verb~|normalization-bench~} repositories, smalltt, elaboration-zoo and the closure-based Lean Kernel Arena checkers teach about building a fast normaliser/conversion checker, and what RLVM should build on GraalVM. Paths are relative to \code{\verb~|refs/~}. Everything marked \strong{\verb~|[~src\verb~|]~} was read in the cited file; \strong{\verb~|[~measured-here\verb~|]~} was measured on this host for this document; \strong{\verb~|[~lit\verb~|]~} is from the literature/general knowledge and was \em{not} verified against source here; \strong{\verb~|[~hyp\verb~|]~} is my interpretation.}

\p{Repositories read (cloned \code{\verb~|--depth 1~}, nothing built or run): \code{\verb~|normalization-bench-guest0x0~} (ed3a61c), \code{\verb~|normalization-bench-kovacs~} (306dad0), \code{\verb~|smalltt~} (ea99b0f), \code{\verb~|elaboration-zoo~}, \code{\verb~|sokonanoda~} (15f5eaa), \code{\verb~|gruszka~} (88aca33), \code{\verb~|nanoclo~}, \code{\verb~|lazylean~} (HEAD, README says 0.5.1), \code{\verb~|cadenza~} (e2b66e2), \code{\verb~|rpylean~}, \code{\verb~|tenet-jvm~}, \code{\verb~|nano-lean~} (README only), \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/checkers/*.yaml~}.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Decisions in one page}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Every one of the fastest arena checkers is NbE over hash-consed values with per-value memo slots} (nano-lean 65.8 s, gruszka 77.0 s, sokonanoda 87.9 s, mathgraph, lazylean HEAD claims 0.48e12 instructions single-threaded). They converged on one design; RLVM should start from it, not from HOAS and not from smalltt's un-shared object graph. Evidence: section 11.}
\li{\strong{HOAS / "compile every lambda to a host closure" buys a small constant on pure beta-reduction and loses every caching lever.} OCaml: compiled-native HOAS is 0.77-1.0x the time of the plain closure interpreter, but compiling costs 0.1-16 s per term (section 3). GHC: interpreter is 1.6-3.3x compiled HOAS (section 2). JVM, measured here on GraalVM CE 25: HOAS with Java lambdas is 0.66-0.87x the CPU of a syntax interpreter in steady state, identical when cold, and 1.0-1.6x \em{slower} without a top-tier JIT (section 4). Host closures are opaque: no structural identity, no \code{\verb~|(term, env)~} cache key, no environment pruning, no pointer-equality shortcut in conversion.}
\li{\strong{Conversion short-cuts matter more than evaluation speed.} smalltt (fast evaluator, no hash-consing, no conversion cache) needs 3.3 s for \code{\verb~|TreeConv23~}; Lean's "slow" substitution kernel answers every TreeConv in 0.001 s because equal terms are the same node (section 2.3). In gruszka, hash-consing values was "the difference between seconds and divergence" on Init (section 8.5).}
\li{\strong{Constants stay folded during evaluation} (glued evaluation without the thunk): \code{\verb~|eval~} does beta/zeta/projection/iota-on-constructor only; delta happens in conversion under Lean's definitional-height order, memoised once per hash-consed application node (section 8.2). This is what makes strict or semi-strict argument evaluation safe.}
\li{\strong{Types are values and the type of a lambda is an "infer closure"}; type inference never quotes. Quoting is needed only by the inductive/recursor checker and for persisting facts across arena resets (section 9).}
\li{\strong{Environment = hash-consed linked list, pruned by a 64-bit free-variable mask} stored on every syntax node; the pruned id is both the closure environment and the cache key (section 6). Linked lists beat AVL maps and skew lists at realistic depths (section 3.2).}
\li{\strong{JVM runtime configuration is a first-order effect on the ranking metric} \verb~|[~measured-here\verb~|]~: for the same NbE workload total process CPU was 2.06 s with SerialGC, 7.46 s with ParallelGC and 10.0 s with G1 on this 20-core host; nursery 32 MB vs 2 GB is another 2.7x; Graal JIT threads add 0.1-0.3 s per process, C2 0.04-0.1 s (section 4). A value heap made of primitive arrays (ids, not objects) removes GC tracing altogether.}
\li{\strong{Deep recursion must be designed out}: both Kovacs (Scala needs \code{\verb~|-Xss1000M~}, JVM Nat-10M times blow up 7-10x for 2x input) and gruszka (1 GiB stack still declined a million-step lazy accumulator until "hand-up" restarts were added) hit it (section 10).}
\li{\strong{Truffle belongs in the closed-computation engine, behind a step budget}, as rpylean (meta-tracing JIT entered only on recursor back-edges) and lazylean/gruszka (separate call-by-need machine for declarations that "compute") do. Mathlib is ~700k small declarations with almost no computation; its instruction count is decided by the constant cost of the hash-consed checker, not by a JIT (section 1.6).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Recommended evaluation core for RLVM}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 Candidate designs, scored (5 = best)}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|#~}\th{Design (exemplars)}\th{Instruction count}\th{Allocation rate}\th{Cacheability}\th{Truffle/JVM risk}\th{Lazy-delta compatibility}}
\tr{\td{A}\td{\strong{Substitution + term caches} (official C++ kernel 33.7 min, tenet-jvm 27.2 min, pestka 4.1 min)}\td{1-2. Guest0x0: 2.5x to >7000x slower than NbE; lazylean's own substitution kernel took 5.5e12 instr on Mathlib vs 0.48e12 after the NbE rewrite (lazylean/README.md)}\td{1. every beta/instantiate rebuilds term spines}\td{4. terms hash-cons well, but caches are per-context (fvars, loose bvars)}\td{5. plain Java, no JIT dependence}\td{5. it \em{is} the reference algorithm}}
\tr{\td{B}\td{\strong{HOAS / compiled closures}: each definition and lambda body becomes a host closure tree or Truffle AST, values are host closures (Coq native_compute, Guest0x0 \code{\verb~|compiled.*~}, Kovacs HOAS, cadenza)}\td{3. best steady-state beta (0.66-0.87x of interpreter on GraalVM, section 4) but compile/JIT cost dominates one-shot terms; conversion and inference are not helped}\td{3. closure objects + boxed args + arg arrays (cadenza README: "Packed argument arrays remain the dominant allocation")}\td{1. closures are opaque: no ids, no \code{\verb~|(term, env)~} keys, no pruning, must apply to compare}\td{1-2. megamorphic call sites, JIT threads charged as instructions, code-cache growth, deopts, Java-stack recursion}\td{2. delta must be suspended explicitly (glued pair per call); cannot compare two closures without running them}}
\tr{\td{C}\td{\strong{Object-graph NbE, smalltt style}: closure = (env, term), linked env, glued \code{\verb~|VUnfold~} thunk, no hash-consing}\td{3. fast eval, but conversion cannot short-circuit (TreeConv23 3.3 s vs 0.001 s)}\td{2. JVM object headers; survivors copied by young GC; GC was 10-65\% of CPU in section 4 depending on nursery}\td{2. pointer identity only; no canonical keys; caches die with the GC graph}\td{4. ordinary Java; deep recursion remains}\td{4. rigid/flex/full maps onto heights with small changes}}
\tr{\td{D}\td{\strong{Hash-consed arena NbE} (sokonanoda, gruszka, lazylean \code{\verb~|nbe.cpp~}, nano-lean): values, spines, environments are ids in flat tables; folded constants; per-value memo slots; pruned environments; sessions}\td{5. all top arena entries}\td{4. 32-40 B flat records, bulk reset, no tracing; one hash probe per node}\td{5. O(1) equality, memo slots, \code{\verb~|(pruned env, term)~} keys, cross-declaration sessions}\td{3. not Truffle-idiomatic (int-indexed heap, nothing for PE to virtualise) but no JIT dependence; bounds checks on every access}\td{5. demonstrated: heights + same-head budgeted probe (sokonanoda/src/checker/conv.rs)}}
\tr{\td{E}\td{\strong{Call-by-need abstract machine with explicit stack} (Krivine/CEK: lazylean machine, rpylean \code{\verb~|_eval.py~}, gruszka \code{\verb~|ground.rs~}, Crégut machines in Guest0x0)}\td{4 on closed computation, 2 on ordinary declarations (heap continuations cost 1.0-3.5x vs recursive NbE in Guest0x0; without hash-consing repeated work is redone: gruszka SuzData 677 G in the machine vs 68 G in the kernel)}\td{3. thunks + frames, but garbage dies young and can be reused in place}\td{2. sharing only through thunk identity}\td{4. closed code with hot loops is exactly Truffle's case (rpylean precedent)}\td{2. produces whnf of closed terms only; conversion stays outside}}
\tr{\td{\strong{F}}\td{\strong{Hybrid: D for all checking + E (optionally Truffle-JIT) for closed computations past a budget} (what lazylean 0.5 and gruszka do)}\td{5}\td{4}\td{5}\td{3}\td{5}}
}

\p{Recommendation: \strong{F}. Build D first (it alone decides the Mathlib number), add E when the arena \code{\verb~|perf~}/\code{\verb~|decide~}-style tests demand it.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.2 Data layout (design D on the JVM)}

\p{Syntax (owned by the export-storage design, summarised here because the evaluator depends on three per-node facts):}

\pre{\verb~|
expr node: kind:u8, a:u32, b:u32, c:u32,
           loose:u16      // number of loose bvars (max index + 1); 0 = closed
           fvMask:u64     // bit i (i<63): de Bruijn index i occurs free; bit 63: some index >= 63
fvMask rules (sokonanoda/src/term/expr.rs:168-268):
  var i          -> 1 << min(i, 63)
  app f a        -> f.mask | a.mask
  lam/pi T b     -> T.mask | under(b)         under(b) = (b.mask >> 1) | (b.loose > 64 ? 1<<63 : 0)
  let T v b      -> T.mask | v.mask | under(b)
  proj s         -> s.mask
  ignoresBinder(body) = (body.mask & 1) == 0   // expr.rs:483
~}

\p{Value heap: one record per value, stored in \code{\verb~|int[]~} chunks (stride 8 = 32 B, gruszka's \code{\verb~|Rec~} is 32 B: \code{\verb~|gruszka/src/tc.rs:532-700~}; sokonanoda's \code{\verb~|VNode~} is 40 B: \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/value.rs:241-253~}). A value is an \code{\verb~|int~} id; id 0 is reserved.}

\pre{\verb~|
word0: kind(4b) | flags(12b) | small(16b)        word4: memo WHNF  (forced value / weak head nf; 0 = unknown)
word1: a                                          word5: memo STEP  (one unfolding, or lambda domain; 0 = unknown, self = "does not step")
word2: b                                          word6: memo TYPE  (inferred type; 0 = unknown)
word3: c                                          word7: spare (digest / generation stamp)

kind      a            b             c            notes
SORT      levelId
NATLIT    bigId                                   STRLIT likewise
LAM       envId        lamExpr                    closure; domain evaluated lazily into STEP
PI        envId        piExpr        domVal       closure for the codomain
PI_INFER  envId        lamExpr       ctxId        "the type of this lambda": domain in STEP, codomain = infer(body) (sec. 9)
THUNK     envId        expr                       delayed argument; WHNF slot holds the forced value
FVAR      level        typeVal                    head; hash-consed by (level, type)
CONST     nameId       levelsId                   head; small = {AXIOM, CTOR, REC, QUOT, IND, UNFOLD}
APP       fnVal        argVal        headVal      spine cell; small/len = spine length
PROJ      structVal    structName    headVal      spine cell; small = field index
flags: CLOSED (no FVAR reachable), HEAD_ACTIVE (head is UNFOLD/REC/QUOT), SPINE_HAS_PROJ
~}

\p{A neutral term is its last spine cell (sokonanoda: "each application node is itself a value", \code{\verb~|value.rs:186-207~}, \code{\verb~|spine()~} at \code{\verb~|value.rs:580-597~}); \code{\verb~|f a b~} and \code{\verb~|f a~} share the \code{\verb~|f a~} cell, so partial applications, spines and neutrals are one mechanism and hash-consing \code{\verb~|(fnVal, argVal)~} gives maximal sharing.}

\p{Environments: a second table, \code{\verb~|env node = (val, parent, len, lsub, mask)~}, hash-consed on \code{\verb~|(parent, val)~}; a third, \code{\verb~|ctx node = (typeVal, parent)~}, lists the types of the bound variables for inference (sokonanoda \code{\verb~|Closure.ctx~}, \code{\verb~|value.rs:82-87~}; \code{\verb~|CtxNode~}, \code{\verb~|value.rs:366-369~}). The base node of a chain carries the universe-level substitution id (\code{\verb~|lsub~}) of the constant whose body is being evaluated, so universe instantiation is never applied to terms (sokonanoda \code{\verb~|EnvNode.lsub~}, \code{\verb~|value.rs:320-329~}; gruszka keeps \code{\verb~|ls~} in every closure instead, \code{\verb~|tc.rs:532-541~}).}

\p{Hash-consing table: open addressing, linear probing, power-of-two \code{\verb~|int[]~} of value ids; key = (word0 without len, a, b, c) compared against the arena words directly (no boxed keys). sokonanoda: \code{\verb~|intern~}, \code{\verb~|value.rs:979-993~}. Head constants are memoised by \code{\verb~|(name, levels)~} (\code{\verb~|eval_const~}, \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/eval.rs:318-331~}).}

\p{Why int ids rather than Java objects: (i) a hash-consed value is reachable from the intern table for the whole session, so with objects every value survives its first young GC and is copied; with \code{\verb~|int[]~} chunks the collector never traces it; (ii) memo-slot writes into old objects dirty GC cards; (iii) session reset is a pointer reset plus table clear; (iv) pair keys pack into one \code{\verb~|long~} (\code{\verb~|(min<<32)|max~}, gruszka \code{\verb~|pair_key~}, \code{\verb~|tc.rs:649~}) for boxing-free caches. Cost: a bounds check per field access. This choice is the largest open question for RLVM and deserves a two-day spike (section 12).}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.3 Evaluation (pseudo-code)}

\pre{\verb~|
eval(env, e):                                   // returns a value id; never unfolds a definition
  if e.kind == VAR: return lookup(env, e.idx)   // no allocation
  if e.loose == 0: return memoClosed[(e, lsub(env))] ?: evalNode(base(lsub(env)), e)
  if e.kind in {APP, PROJ, LET, PI, LAM}:
      env = prune(env, e.fvMask)                // canonical key, section 6.3
      return memoOpen[(env, e)] ?: evalNode(env, e)
  return evalNode(env, e)

evalNode(env, e) = switch e.kind:
  SORT l      -> mkSort(simplify(substLevel(l, lsub(env))))
  CONST c ls  -> constHead(c, substLevels(ls, lsub(env)))          // UNFOLD head if c is a def/thm
  NATLIT/STR  -> mkLit
  LAM         -> mkLam(prune(env, e.fvMask), e)
  PI          -> mkPi(prune(env, e.fvMask), e, eval(env, e.dom))
  LET         -> loop over the let-chain: env = cons(env, arg(env, val)); e = body      // iterative
  PROJ i s    -> proj(i, eval(env, s))
  APP         -> (head, a1..an) = peelSpine(e)                      // iterative, args on a small stack
                 f = eval(env, head)
                 if f is REC/QUOT head: evaluate all args, try to fire once (major already a ctor/literal)
                 k = 0
                 while k < n:
                   if f.kind == LAM:            // multi-argument beta without intermediate closures
                       benv = f.env; body = f.lamExpr
                       repeat: benv = cons(benv, arg(env, a[k++])); body = body.body
                       until k == n or body is not a lambda
                       f = eval(benv, body)
                   else f = app(f, arg(env, a[k++]))
                 return f

arg(env, a):                                    // argument policy, section 7
  if a.kind in {VAR, CONST, SORT, LIT, LAM}: return eval(env, a)   // already a value or O(1)
  if callee's binder ignores it (ignoresBinder): return HOLE        // never evaluated
  return mkThunk(prune(env, a.fvMask), a)       // hash-consed (expr, env): also the open-eval cache entry

app(f, x):
  switch f.kind:
    LAM            -> eval(cons(f.env, x), f.lamExpr.body)
    FVAR/CONST/APP/PROJ -> mkApp(f, x)          // hash-consed spine extension; Nat.succ on a literal folds
    THUNK          -> app(force(f), x)
    else           -> reject("ill-typed application")

force(v):  while v.kind == THUNK: { w = WHNF[v]; if w == 0 { w = eval(v.env, v.expr); WHNF[v] = w }; v = w }; return v
~}

\p{The multi-argument beta loop and the "walk a call's lambdas as (term, env) pairs, create a value only for the result" rule are in \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/eval.rs:219-257, 427-450~} and gruszka \code{\verb~|docs/architecture.md~} ("What is not computed"). The strict/thunk switch in \code{\verb~|arg~} should be a compile-time flag for the first measurements: sokonanoda is fully strict (\code{\verb~|eval.rs:238-256~}), gruszka uses thunks (\code{\verb~|Val::Thunk~}, \code{\verb~|tc.rs:541~}).}

\p{Weak-head normalisation is an iterative loop with an explicit stack of pending eliminators; recursion is only used to evaluate bodies:}

\pre{\verb~|
whnf(v):                                        // sokonanoda force_reducible, eval/reduction.rs:162-216
  if WHNF[v] != 0: return it
  cur = v; waiting = []                         // explicit stack: recursors/projections awaiting their major
  loop:
    cur = force(cur)
    if head(cur) is UNFOLD: nxt = unfold(cur); if nxt != cur { cur = nxt; continue }
    else if head(cur) is REC or QUOT (enough args):
        m = major(cur)
        if m is ctor app / literal: { cur = fire(cur, m); continue }
        if m can still reduce:   { waiting.push(cur); cur = m; continue }      // "Descend"
    else if cur is PROJ on a reducible struct: { waiting.push(cur); cur = struct; continue }
    // cur is canonical or stuck
    if waiting.empty: break
    p = waiting.pop(); r = fire(p, cur)
    if r != none { STEP[p] = r; cur = r } else { STEP[p] = p; cur = p }
  WHNF[v] = cur; return cur

unfold(v):                                      // sokonanoda unfold_value_go, eval/reduction.rs:373-441
  if STEP[v] != 0: return STEP[v]
  body = STEP[headConst] ?: eval(base(lsub = (uparams -> levels)), defValueExpr)   // once per (const, levels)
  r = applyMany(body, spine args and projections in order)                          // multi-arg beta
  STEP[v] = r; return r
~}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.4 Conversion (pseudo-code; Lean rules on values)}

\pre{\verb~|
conv(d, x, y, RIGID):                           // d = current de Bruijn level; RIGID=false is "flex/probe" mode
  if x == y: return true                        // hash-consing: identity
  if cacheable(kind x or y): hit posCache[(min,max)] -> true
                             RIGID and neither is LAM: hit negCache -> false
  if probing: if budget-- == 0 { exhausted = true; return false }
  if natShortcut(x, y) decides: return it       // literal vs succ/zero forms
  r = direct(d, x, y, RIGID) or (RIGID and cold(d, x, y))
  record: true -> posCache; false -> negCache only if RIGID, no LAM, and not exhausted
  return r

direct:
  SORT/SORT        -> level equivalence          NATLIT/NATLIT, STRLIT/STRLIT -> same id
  FVAR-headed, same head                         -> spines(all positions)
  CTOR/IND/AXIOM-headed, same name, equivalent levels -> spines(skipping ignorable positions, 8.3)
  REC/QUOT-headed  -> RIGID: same head and spineProbe -> true; else proof-irrelevance; else iota either
                      side and retry; else same-head spines.      flex: same head -> spines, else false
  PI/PI, LAM/LAM   -> same (bodyExpr, env) and same domain -> true        // closure identity
                      else conv(domains) then conv(d+1, body1 @ fv, body2 @ fv) with fv = mkFVar(d, dom)
  UNFOLD/UNFOLD    -> flex: same head -> spines, else false
                      RIGID, same head: spineProbe (budget per argument pair) -> true
                                        else proof-irrelevance -> true; else unfold both, retry
                      RIGID, different heads: compare hints; unfold the side with the larger height
                                        (abbrev > regular(h) > opaque); equal heights: unfold both
  UNFOLD/other     -> RIGID: proof-irrelevance, else unfold the UNFOLD side and retry; flex: false
  stuck REC/other  -> RIGID: proof-irrelevance, iota, unfold the other side
cold (rare, kept out of the hot function as smalltt does with unifyCold):
  proof irrelevance (typeOf both, both types in Prop, types convertible)
  eta: LAM vs non-LAM -> compare body @ fv with app(other, fv) at d+1
  structure eta / unit-like: typeOf both, whnf type is a structure S: unit-like -> true;
                             one side is S.mk args -> conv(proj_i(other), arg_i) for every field
~}

\p{Sources: \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/conv.rs:86-450~} (direct), \code{\verb~|:501-535~} (probe), \code{\verb~|:690-838~} (cold), hints \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/env.rs:14-35~}; smalltt's original three states \code{\verb~|smalltt/src/Unification.hs:321-382~}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.5 Thresholds found in the sources (starting values for RLVM)}

\table{
\tr{\th{Constant}\th{Value}\th{Where}}
\tr{\td{same-head speculative probe budget}\td{2048 conv steps per argument pair}\td{sokonanoda \code{\verb~|conv.rs:134~} (\code{\verb~|PROBE_CAP~})}}
\tr{\td{same-head argument pre-probe}\td{256 ticks per pair, last argument first}\td{gruszka architecture.md, milestone 36}}
\tr{\td{outer lazy-delta budget before trying other strategies}\td{4096 ticks, doubling per round}\td{gruszka \code{\verb~|HEAD_PROBE~} / \code{\verb~|RACE_B0~}}}
\tr{\td{smalltt speculation}\td{exactly one flex attempt per path, then full}\td{smalltt README "Approximate conversion checking"}}
\tr{\td{free-variable mask width}\td{63 exact indices + 1 "far" bit}\td{sokonanoda \code{\verb~|expr.rs:168-169~}}}
\tr{\td{session size (arena + caches kept across declarations)}\td{8 MiB of tables (gruszka), 9 MiB budget (sokonanoda \code{\verb~|cache.rs:13~}), 128 MB (lazylean, Mathlib)}\td{resp. docs}}
\tr{\td{persistent closed-whnf store admission}\td{value seen twice (\code{\verb~|WHNF_ADMIT_THRESHOLD = 2~})}\td{sokonanoda \code{\verb~|eval/whnf_cache.rs:22~}}}
\tr{\td{"this declaration computes" switch to the lazy machine}\td{> 1 M evaluation steps or > 64 MB of values (lazylean); session holds 2^26 values (gruszka \code{\verb~|GROUND_AT~})}\td{READMEs}}
\tr{\td{reclaim inside a declaration}\td{regions open at 2^22 values; roll back if result reaches <= 1/4 of >= 256 new values}\td{gruszka "Regions"}}
\tr{\td{nested-whnf depth before handing work up}\td{8192 (\code{\verb~|DEEP_WHNF~}) or half the stack}\td{gruszka "Deep whnfs handed up"}}
\tr{\td{derive a rewrite rule for a hot recursive definition}\td{32 unfoldings in a session (\code{\verb~|RULE_HOT~})}\td{gruszka "Derived rules"}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.6 Where Truffle fits}

\ul{
\li{The checker (design D) is a data-structure program: its hot loops are hash probes, spine walks and memo-slot reads. Partial evaluation has no guest program to specialise on, because the "program" is a different small term for each of ~700k declarations. Run it as ordinary Java compiled by the host JIT; write it allocation-free (no boxed keys, no iterator objects, no lambdas in hot paths) so that JIT and GC threads stay idle.}
\li{The closed-computation engine (design E) is where a Truffle AST or Bytecode-DSL interpreter pays: closed terms, hot recursive definitions, values that die young. Precedents: rpylean compiles each Lean expression once into an immutable \code{\verb~|CodeNode~} graph, uses \code{\verb~|(rec_edge, code)~} as JIT green variables and enters the JIT \em{only} on recursive recursor back-edges; a stuck neutral raises \code{\verb~|_EvalBail~} and the general machine takes over (\code{\verb~|rpylean/rpylean/_eval.py:1-18, 73, 1421-1423, 1578, 1994~}). cadenza (Truffle, Kotlin) represents neutrals as a \code{\verb~|SlowPathException~} subclass thrown out of compiled code (\code{\verb~|cadenza/src/cadenza/data/NeutralException.kt:6~}) and dispatches closure calls through a 3-entry \code{\verb~|DirectCallNode~} inline cache falling back to \code{\verb~|IndirectCallNode~} (\code{\verb~|cadenza/src/cadenza/jit/dispatch.kt:24-41, 48-112~}).}
\li{Entry rule for E (from lazylean/gruszka/rpylean): the term is closed, the plain engine has already spent a step budget on it, and the consumer only needs a weak-head normal form or a comparison against a constructor/literal. Anything E cannot do returns the whole run to D.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Evidence A: AndrasKovacs/normalization-bench}

\p{Workload: untyped Church numerals (\code{\verb~|n5M~}, \code{\verb~|n10M~} built by \code{\verb~|mul~}) and full binary Church trees (\code{\verb~|fullTree n20..n22~}, 2M-8M nodes); "normalization" = eval + quote, "conversion" = beta conversion check of two differently-built equal values. Machine: i7-3770, 8 GB, Ubuntu 16.04. Scala 2.13.2 on OpenJDK 1.8.0_232 with \code{\verb~|-Xss1000M -Xmx4G~}; "graalvm" = GraalVM 21.0.0.2, same options. GHC 8.8.1 \code{\verb~|-O2 -fllvm~}, \code{\verb~|+RTS -A1G~}. Times in ms, average of 20 runs (Coq and smalltt: single run). Reproduced from \code{\verb~|normalization-bench-kovacs/README.md:94-105~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{GHC HOAS CBV}\th{GHC HOAS CBN}\th{GHC interp CBV}\th{OCaml HOAS}\th{nodejs HOAS}\th{Scala HOAS}\th{Scala HOAS graalvm}\th{F\verb~|#~ HOAS}\th{Coq cbv}\th{Coq lazy}\th{Coq vm_compute}\th{Coq native_compute}\th{smalltt}\th{luajit}}
\tr{\td{Nat 5M conversion}\td{90}\td{112}\td{234}\td{268}\td{700}\td{138}\td{1013}\td{1246}\td{N/A}\td{31663}\td{1208}\td{3359}\td{500}\td{stack overflow}}
\tr{\td{Nat 5M normalization}\td{101}\td{108}\td{167}\td{131}\td{976}\td{374}\td{2031}\td{69592}\td{1507}\td{2604}\td{4309}\td{4790}\td{411}\td{stack overflow}}
\tr{\td{Nat 10M conversion}\td{208}\td{224}\td{695}\td{610}\td{1395}\td{960}\td{2145}\td{4462}\td{N/A}\td{OOM}\td{6965}\td{5081}\td{1681}\td{stack overflow}}
\tr{\td{Nat 10M normalization}\td{227}\td{269}\td{439}\td{965}\td{3718}\td{3908}\td{5833}\td{too long}\td{3340}\td{5780}\td{11216}\td{13181}\td{1148}\td{stack overflow}}
\tr{\td{Tree 2M conversion}\td{136}\td{114}\td{274}\td{117}\td{396}\td{118}\td{137}\td{305}\td{N/A}\td{477}\td{561}\td{691}\td{425}\td{3576}}
\tr{\td{Tree 2M normalization}\td{86}\td{76}\td{163}\td{244}\td{323}\td{85}\td{84}\td{1514}\td{1248}\td{702}\td{960}\td{1103}\td{346}\td{2533}}
\tr{\td{Tree 4M conversion}\td{294}\td{229}\td{588}\td{150}\td{827}\td{271}\td{250}\td{630}\td{N/A}\td{646}\td{1276}\td{1302}\td{1429}\td{OOM}}
\tr{\td{Tree 4M normalization}\td{192}\td{194}\td{343}\td{534}\td{635}\td{181}\td{164}\td{3119}\td{1365}\td{1488}\td{1729}\td{1983}\td{745}\td{OOM}}
\tr{\td{Tree 8M conversion}\td{723}\td{457}\td{1268}\td{253}\td{1726}\td{625}\td{516}\td{1232}\td{N/A}\td{1279}\td{2420}\td{2901}\td{2371}\td{OOM}}
\tr{\td{Tree 8M normalization}\td{436}\td{525}\td{716}\td{1298}\td{1398}\td{731}\td{332}\td{5930}\td{3275}\td{2871}\td{3464}\td{3497}\td{1544}\td{OOM}}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 The variants}

\table{
\tr{\th{Variant}\th{What it is \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~}}
\tr{\td{GHC HOAS CBV (\code{\verb~|haskell/HOASCBV.hs~})}\td{\code{\verb~|data Val = VLam !(Val -> Val) | VVar !Int | VApp !Val !Val~}; benchmark terms are written \em{as Haskell lambdas}, i.e. compiled HOAS with zero interpretive overhead; \code{\verb~|VLam f $$ a = seq a (f a)~}; values use de Bruijn levels, \code{\verb~|quote~} converts to indices}}
\tr{\td{GHC HOAS CBN (\code{\verb~|haskell/HOASCBN.hs~})}\td{identical minus the \code{\verb~|seq~} and with a lazy argument field: call-by-need through GHC thunks}}
\tr{\td{GHC interp CBV (\code{\verb~|haskell/Interp.hs~})}\td{syntax \code{\verb~|Local Int | App | Lam | Top Int~}, top-level definitions in an array, closures \code{\verb~|VLam Env Tm~}, environment = linked list \code{\verb~|Nil | Skip Env | Def Env Val~}; module-wide \code{\verb~|Strict~}}}
\tr{\td{OCaml / nodejs / F\verb~|#~ / Scala HOAS}\td{the same deep HOAS; Scala: \code{\verb~|case class VLam(t: Val => Val)~} (\code{\verb~|scala/normalization-bench/HOAS.scala:20-23~})}}
\tr{\td{Coq cbv / lazy / vm_compute / native_compute}\td{typed impredicative Church encodings, conversion by \code{\verb~|eq_refl~} (\code{\verb~|coq/Bench.v~}); \code{\verb~|cbv~} and \code{\verb~|lazy~} are the kernel's abstract machines, \code{\verb~|vm_compute~} the ZINC-derived bytecode VM, \code{\verb~|native_compute~} compilation to OCaml \verb~|[~lit\verb~|]~}}
\tr{\td{smalltt 0.2}\td{elaborator with glued evaluation (section 8.1), interpreted}}
}

\p{Caveat \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~: the OCaml tree workload is not the same term. \code{\verb~|ocaml/HOASCBV.ml:39-40~} defines \code{\verb~|node~} as \code{\verb~|fun t1 t2 l n -> n t1 t2~}, whereas Haskell/Scala use \code{\verb~|n (t1 l n) (t2 l n)~}. The OCaml "Tree" columns therefore measure a different computation and should not be compared.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 Why the numbers look like this}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Interpreter vs compiled HOAS is a small constant}: GHC interp/HOAS = 1.6-3.3x (234/90, 167/101, 695/208, 274/136, 163/86, 716/436). Kovacs' conclusion: "plain hand-written interpreters ... have comparable performance to JIT HOAS" (\code{\verb~|README.md:183-191~}).}
\li{\strong{CBN vs CBV is noise in GHC} (0.63x-1.24x) because thunks are native there; on any other runtime a thunk is an object plus a check (Guest0x0 measures 1.1-2x, section 3.2).}
\li{\strong{JVM HOAS (C2) equals GHC on trees} (118 vs 136, 85 vs 86, 625 vs 723 ms) \strong{but degrades super-linearly on deep numerals}: Nat conversion 138 -> 960 ms and normalization 374 -> 3908 ms for 2x input. \code{\verb~|quote~}/\code{\verb~|conv~} recurse once per \code{\verb~|VApp~}, i.e. 5-10 million frames deep. Kovacs: "perhaps there is some issue with deep stacks"; also "sometimes I got a sudden memory hike which resulted in out-of-memory process termination". \verb~|[~hyp\verb~|]~ HotSpot's stop-the-world collectors scan the whole thread stack as roots at every young collection, so GC cost grows with stack depth times number of collections.}
\li{\strong{GraalVM (21.0) vs C2}: trees equal or better (Tree 8M normalization 332 vs 731 ms, the best of any system), deep numerals 1.5-7x worse (1013 vs 138 ms). So Graal's closure inlining/escape analysis helps wide shallow workloads and its deep-recursion behaviour was worse than C2's. Only JVM-on-Graal data point in either repository; it is JIT-compiled Scala, not a Truffle interpreter.}
\li{\strong{Memory headroom is a 3-6x factor}: "GHC solutions & smalltt had 3-6 times speedup from \code{\verb~|-A1G~}. Scala and nodejs performance becomes dismal without free RAM" (\code{\verb~|README.md:157-161~}). Confirmed for the JVM in section 4 (2.7x).}
\li{\strong{Bytecode VMs and "native" compilation in a proof assistant are not automatically fast}: Coq \code{\verb~|vm_compute~} is 3-50x slower than GHC HOAS here, and \code{\verb~|native_compute~} is not better than \code{\verb~|vm_compute~} (3359 vs 1208, 4790 vs 4309).}
\li{\strong{All JIT entries are CBV}; Kovacs warns that call-by-need "could possibly be a significant performance hit on JVM" and did not measure it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 smalltt's own tables (\code{\verb~|smalltt/README.md:756-805~}), seconds}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{smalltt}\th{Agda}\th{Coq}\th{Lean 4 (2021-11)}\th{Idris 2}}
\tr{\td{NatConv 1M / 5M / 10M}\td{0.045 / 0.188 / 0.712}\td{1.8 / 9.6 / 19.7}\td{stack overflow}\td{16.4 / 34.6 / 61.1}\td{3.22 / 29.65 / 173.88}}
\tr{\td{TreeConv 20 / 23}\td{0.408 / 3.325}\td{1.7 / 13.7}\td{0.618 / 4.699}\td{0.001 / 0.001}\td{16.65 / 25.38}}
\tr{\td{ForceTree 23}\td{4.372}\td{15.93}\td{vm 0.731, compute 5.407, lazy 9.664}\td{reduce 62.7, eval 5.52}\td{OOM}}
\tr{\td{NfTree 23}\td{3.023}\td{N/A}\td{vm 5.996, compute 4.99, lazy 7.187}\td{N/A}\td{N/A}}
}

\p{Reading: (1) NbE beats the substitution kernel by 85-360x when real reduction is needed (NatConv). (2) Lean wins TreeConv by 3000x with no evaluation at all, because both sides are structurally shared and the kernel compares/caches by node identity; smalltt has neither hash-consing nor a conversion cache and walks 2^23 nodes. A Lean kernel workload is far closer to TreeConv than to NatConv, hence decision 3. (3) "smalltt with glued evaluation disabled would be 2x faster" on ForceTree (\code{\verb~|README.md:809-812~}): glued thunks are not free.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Evidence B: Guest0x0/normalization-bench (OCaml)}

\p{Framework: de Bruijn-index input terms, wall time of normalisation only, fresh process per run, 20 s timeout (\code{\verb~|MISSING~}), laptop, GC untuned (author's caveats, \code{\verb~|README.md:319-352~}). Benchmarks (\code{\verb~|bench.ml:68-130~}, \code{\verb~|Common/Terms.ml~}): \code{\verb~|church_add n n~}, \code{\verb~|church_mul n n~}, \code{\verb~|exponential n~} (\code{\verb~|\x. t(n)~}, \code{\verb~|t(k+1) = (\y. y y) t(k)~}: normal form of size 2^n, fully shared), \code{\verb~|parigot_add~} (Parigot numerals, exponential normal form with sharing), \code{\verb~|random~} (50 uniformly random terms of the given size), \code{\verb~|self_interp_size~} (Scott-encoded term, structural recursion computing its size).}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 The normalisers \verb~|[~src: \code{\verb~|Normalizers/*.ml~}\verb~|]~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Name}\th{Technique}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|subst.naive~}, \code{\verb~|subst.whead~}}\td{capture-avoiding substitution with explicit \code{\verb~|shift~}; strong-CBV order vs weak-head-first (\code{\verb~|Subst.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.closure.list~} / \code{\verb~|.tree~}}\td{defunctionalised closures \code{\verb~|VLam of value list * term~}; env = OCaml list (\code{\verb~|List.nth~}) or AVL \code{\verb~|Map~} keyed by level (\code{\verb~|NBE_Closure.ml~}). \strong{Reference baseline.}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.HOAS.list/tree/skew~}}\td{\code{\verb~|VLam of (value -> value)~}: host closures created \em{by the interpreter} at each \code{\verb~|Lam~}; env list / AVL / Okasaki skew-binary random-access list (\code{\verb~|NBE_HOAS.ml~}, \code{\verb~|Common/Data.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.named.list/tree/ADT~}}\td{named variables, global fresh counter; env = assoc list / AVL / assoc list with pairs inlined into the cons cell (\code{\verb~|NBE_Named.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.pushenter~}}\td{separate argument stack, closures allocated only when the stack is empty (\code{\verb~|NBE_Pushenter.ml~}); not in the result set}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.lazy~}}\td{\code{\verb~|Lazy.t~} around every value: call-by-need everywhere (\code{\verb~|NBE_Lazy.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.memo.v1..v4~}, \code{\verb~|.named~}}\td{closure NbE where every value has \strong{one} memo slot for the term it quotes to at one level. v1: record + \code{\verb~|(int*term) option~} (2-3 indirections); v2: record with two mutable fields; v3: slot inlined in each constructor block (1 indirection); v4 = v3 without slots on variables; named: output is level-independent so the slot never misses (\code{\verb~|NBE_Memo.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.HC.idx(.o)~}, \code{\verb~|.lvlidx(.o)~}}\td{pre-allocated arrays of the 10000 \code{\verb~|Idx i~} leaves (and \code{\verb~|VLvl i~}), i.e. leaf sharing only; \code{\verb~|.o~} = output only}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|NBE.HC.hashcons~}}\td{fully hash-consed \em{terms} (input and quoted output) via \code{\verb~|Hashtbl~} with stored hashes; values not hash-consed, nothing memoised (\code{\verb~|NBE_HashConsing.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|AM.Crégut.list/ADT/arr~}}\td{Crégut's strongly-reducing Krivine machine (CBN, no sharing); continuation stack as list of frames / frames with the tail inlined / preallocated array (\code{\verb~|AbstractMachine.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|AM.Crégut.CBV~}}\td{author's CBV variant with heap continuations}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|compiled.HOAS.{byte,native,O2}~}}\td{term printed as an OCaml program in tagged HOAS, compiled by \code{\verb~|ocamlc~}/\code{\verb~|ocamlopt~}, then run (\code{\verb~|Compiled_HOAS.ml~})}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|compiled.evalapply.*~}}\td{the same with n-ary \code{\verb~|VLam1..VLam5~} and \code{\verb~|app1..app5~} (eval/apply uncurrying) (\code{\verb~|Compiled_Evalapply.ml~})}}
}
}

