\subtree{
\title{3.3 Why (author's analysis \code{\verb~|README.md:354-761~}, plus my reading of the code)}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Substitution loses by 2.5x to >7000x} because each beta step allocates a substituted copy; NbE defers and merges substitutions in the environment.}
\li{\strong{Environment data structure}: plain list wins everywhere; AVL is up to 7.7x slower (self_interp 0.0709 vs 0.0092), skew list 1.0-2.2x slower. Reasons: \code{\verb~|cons~} is the most frequent operation and is O(1)/one allocation only for the list; lookups mostly hit recent variables. Named variables with association lists are up to 5x slower than indices (random 8000: 0.0071 vs 0.0014). Author's caveat: environments in these benchmarks are shallow.}
\li{\strong{Interpreter-created HOAS closures = defunctionalised closures} (within noise); the only difference is an indirect call instead of a direct call to \code{\verb~|eval~}.}
\li{\strong{Laziness everywhere costs 1.1-2.0x} (church_add 100k 0.0619 vs 0.0385, random 0.00315 vs 0.00169) when it saves nothing.}
\li{\strong{Heap-allocated continuations cost 1.0-3.5x} over using the host stack (\code{\verb~|AM.Crégut.CBV~} vs \code{\verb~|closure.list~}: church_add 100k 2.2x, random 3.5x). Inlining the stack tail into each frame beats a list of frames (one indirection per transition instead of two) and beats a bounds-checked array.}
\li{\strong{The \code{\verb~|AM.Crégut.CBV~} exponential result (7 microseconds) is a sharing effect, and the machine is wrong in general} \verb~|[~my reading of \code{\verb~|AbstractMachine.ml:125-163~}; not executed\verb~|]~: neutral applications are turned into already-quoted \code{\verb~|VNF term~} values the moment they are built and are then passed around by reference, so the 2^24-node normal form is a 24-node DAG that is never traversed. But the de Bruijn indices inside a \code{\verb~|VNF~} are fixed at the level where it was created; using it under more binders (e.g. \code{\verb~|\x. (\y. \z. y) (x x)~}) would give wrong indices. Lesson kept: \em{emit shared DAGs, never trees}; lesson rejected: index-based results cannot be reused across levels without a shift or level-based output.}
\li{\strong{One-slot quote memo}: with the slot inlined (v3) the overhead on sharing-free inputs is 1.1-1.8x and the gain on shared results is 8.6x (parigot_add 12) to 5e5x (exponential). Layout dominates: v1 (two extra indirections) costs 1.2-5.2x. Caching leaves too (v3 vs v4) is \em{faster} because it avoids allocation. With level-independent (named) output the memo never misses: parigot_add 12 in 97 microseconds, 61000x faster than the baseline.}
\li{\strong{Sharing leaf nodes} is a free 1.2-1.45x on church_add. \strong{Full hash-consing of terms without any memoisation} costs 1.5-15x when there is no sharing (random 8000: 0.054 vs 0.0035; church_mul 4.3x) and wins only 2.4-4.4x when there is. Hash-consing pays when it is used as a key for memoisation and identity tests, not as a goal in itself.}
\li{\strong{Compilation}: native compiled HOAS runs at 0.77-1.0x of the interpreter on the three benchmarks with real work and 3.4x \em{slower} on \code{\verb~|random 1000~} (bytecode 1.3-1.7x slower throughout); eval/apply uncurrying and \code{\verb~|-O2~} make no measurable difference. Native compile time is 0.1-0.2 s minimum and 3-16 s (bytecode 0.7-10.6 s) for a 1000-4000-node random term whose interpretation takes 0.4-3.7 ms. A term must be evaluated >= 10^4 times before compiling it pays.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Evidence C: the same question on GraalVM CE 25 \verb~|[~measured-here\verb~|]~}

\p{Scratch program (not in the repository; \code{\verb~|/tmp/rlvm-nbe-spike/Nbe.java~}, ~140 lines) running Kovacs' workloads (Tree 2M normalization / conversion, Nat 1M conversion) with three cores:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A = HOAS}: \code{\verb~|VLam(Function<Val,Val>)~}, terms written as Java lambdas (as in the Scala file).}
\li{\strong{B = syntax interpreter}: \code{\verb~|eval(env, tm)~} with an \code{\verb~|instanceof~} chain, \code{\verb~|VLam(env, tm)~}, linked \code{\verb~|Env(val, next)~}.}
\li{\strong{C = node tree ("closure generation")}: term precompiled once to objects with a virtual \code{\verb~|exec(env)~} (what a Truffle AST looks like before partial evaluation), same values.}
}

\p{Host: aarch64, 20 cores, \code{\verb~|graalvm-ce-25~} (JDK 25.0.5), \code{\verb~|-Xms6g -Xmx6g~}, worker thread with a 2 GB stack. Metric: \strong{total process CPU time in ms} (all threads: JIT + GC + mutator), read from \code{\verb~|OperatingSystemMXBean.getProcessCpuTime()~}; \code{\verb~|perf~} is unavailable on this host (\code{\verb~|perf_event_paranoid = 4~}), so this is a proxy for the arena's instruction count. One run per cell; a repeat of three cells agreed within 3\%.}

\table{
\tr{\th{JIT configuration}\th{core}\th{treeNf x20}\th{treeConv x20}\th{natConv x20}\th{treeNf x1}\th{treeConv x1}\th{natConv x1}}
\tr{\td{Graal (default), SerialGC}\td{A HOAS}\td{1420}\td{1700}\td{930}\td{360}\td{380}\td{390}}
\tr{\td{}\td{B interp}\td{1790}\td{2570}\td{1070}\td{270}\td{350}\td{250}}
\tr{\td{}\td{C node tree}\td{1450}\td{1960}\td{990}\td{280}\td{380}\td{250}}
\tr{\td{C2 (\code{\verb~|-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~}), SerialGC}\td{A HOAS}\td{1530}\td{1740}\td{830}\td{240}\td{260}\td{180}}
\tr{\td{}\td{B interp}\td{1820}\td{2570}\td{1080}\td{270}\td{340}\td{190}}
\tr{\td{}\td{C node tree}\td{1940}\td{2710}\td{1160}\td{260}\td{350}\td{210}}
\tr{\td{C1 only (\code{\verb~|-XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1~}), SerialGC}\td{A HOAS}\td{3230}\td{5080}\td{1290}\td{280}\td{400}\td{220}}
\tr{\td{}\td{B interp}\td{2210}\td{3170}\td{1270}\td{190}\td{300}\td{160}}
\tr{\td{}\td{C node tree}\td{2220}\td{3240}\td{1240}\td{210}\td{280}\td{170}}
}

\p{JIT compiler time inside those totals: Graal 76-336 ms, C2 39-100 ms, C1 18-54 ms.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Collector, core C, treeConv x20}\th{total CPU ms}\th{reported GC ms}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC~}}\td{2060}\td{176}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseParallelGC -XX:ParallelGCThreads=1~}}\td{2690}\td{651}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseParallelGC~} (default thread count)}\td{7460}\td{370}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseG1GC~} (JVM default)}\td{10020}\td{509}}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{Young generation size, core B, x20, SerialGC}\th{treeNf CPU (GC) ms}\th{natNf CPU (GC) ms}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-Xmn32m~}}\td{4770 (3076)}\td{2050 (1171)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-Xmn256m~}}\td{2640 (1344)}\td{1010 (216)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-Xmn2g~}}\td{1740 (162)}\td{940 (11)}}
}

\p{Findings:}

\ol{
\li{With a top-tier JIT, HOAS costs 0.66-0.87x of the syntax interpreter and the node tree sits between them: the same order as OCaml (0.77-1.0x) and GHC (0.3-0.6x). It is a constant factor on the beta-reduction part only.}
\li{Cold (one pass over a 2M-node workload, about 0.1 s of mutator work) the three cores are indistinguishable and JIT threads are 25-65\% of process CPU with Graal (13-40\% with C2). Kernel checking of Mathlib is mostly cold code paths over fresh terms.}
\li{Without a top tier (C1 only) HOAS is 1.0-1.6x \em{slower} than the interpreter: the megamorphic \code{\verb~|Function.apply~} call is never inlined and lambda objects are not scalar-replaced.}
\li{Parallel and concurrent collectors multiply \em{total} CPU by 3.6-4.9x on a many-core host for identical mutator work. The arena charges every thread. Use SerialGC (or a single GC thread) and a large young generation, and prefer data that the GC does not trace.}
\li{Nursery size is a 2.2-2.7x factor (the JVM version of Kovacs' \code{\verb~|-A1G~} observation).}
\li{The Graal JIT's fixed cost is 2-3x C2's for this small program; both are negligible against a budget of a few hundred CPU-seconds \em{as long as the set of hot methods stays small} (a hand-written kernel), and not negligible if thousands of guest definitions were compiled.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. HOAS on the JVM and on Truffle}

\p{How a host lambda executes on the JVM \verb~|[~lit, standard JVM behaviour\verb~|]~: each lambda expression becomes one hidden class via \code{\verb~|invokedynamic~}/\code{\verb~|LambdaMetafactory~} (Scala >= 2.12 and Java); capturing lambdas allocate one object per evaluation holding the captured variables (a flat closure); application is \code{\verb~|invokeinterface Function.apply~}. A normaliser has one \code{\verb~|ap~} function through which \em{all} closures are applied, so that call site sees every lambda class: beyond two receiver types C2 emits a vtable/itable call with no inlining; Graal inlines through polymorphic sites more aggressively and scalar-replaces non-escaping closures, which is consistent with its tree results (332 ms vs 731 ms for C2, section 2) and with section 4.}

\p{Three ways to host closures, and what each costs a \em{kernel}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Host lambdas / generated classes}\th{Tree of executable nodes ("closure generation", Truffle AST)}\th{Syntax + environment (defunctionalised)}}
\tr{\td{Creation cost per Lean lambda}\td{class spin-up + JIT (ms each); 700k declarations make this prohibitive}\td{one object per syntax node, once per definition}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{Application}\td{megamorphic interface call}\td{virtual \code{\verb~|exec~}, monomorphic per node after inlining; with Truffle a \code{\verb~|DirectCallNode~} inline cache per call site (cadenza uses limit 3, then \code{\verb~|IndirectCallNode~})}\td{direct call to \code{\verb~|eval~} + kind switch}}
\tr{\td{Identity / hashing of a closure}\td{none (opaque object)}\td{\code{\verb~|(node, env)~} if envs are comparable}\td{\code{\verb~|(expr id, env id)~}: an integer pair}}
\tr{\td{Environment pruning, free-variable mask}\td{no}\td{possible (frame descriptor)}\td{yes, a field read}}
\tr{\td{Readback / quote}\td{must apply to a fresh variable and re-run the body}\td{same}\td{same, but memoisable by \code{\verb~|(value, level)~}}}
\tr{\td{Comparing two closures}\td{must run both}\td{must run both, or compare \code{\verb~|(node, env)~}}\td{\code{\verb~|bodyExpr == bodyExpr' && env == env'~} then run}}
\tr{\td{Partial evaluation benefit}\td{-}\td{large when the \em{same} guest function is hot and its callees are constants (closed computation)}\td{none needed}}
}

\p{Truffle-specific facts relevant to the design:}

\ul{
\li{PE removes interpretive overhead only for call targets that become hot, and it virtualises frames/closures only if they do not escape. In NbE nearly every closure and environment escapes into the value heap, and open terms force a second path. cadenza's answer is to make closed evaluation the only compiled path and to \em{throw} neutrals out of it (\code{\verb~|NeutralException extends SlowPathException~}); rpylean's answer is \code{\verb~|_EvalBail~}.}
\li{A closure in a Truffle language is \code{\verb~|(RootCallTarget, captured env, partial args, arity)~} (\code{\verb~|cadenza/src/cadenza/data/Closure.kt:27-34~}); calls need eval/apply-style arity dispatch (\code{\verb~|dispatch.kt:48-112~}: exact, over-applied, under-applied -> PAP, megamorphic fallback) and a tail-call trampoline. cadenza's README records that packed argument arrays are the dominant allocation of its recursive benchmark: the Truffle calling convention itself allocates.}
\li{Compilation is asynchronous on compiler threads whose instructions the arena counts. Thresholds and thread counts are engine options and must be pinned (not verified here; see the Truffle platform document). rpylean restricts JIT entry to recursor back-edges so that "non-looping type checking pays negligible JIT bookkeeping overhead" (\code{\verb~|_eval.py:13-17~}).}
\li{Readback is where HOAS hurts most: every quote or conversion under a binder re-executes the closure body on a fresh variable. With defunctionalised closures this can be cached by \code{\verb~|(body, env + fresh var)~} because fresh variables are hash-consed by \code{\verb~|(level, type)~} (section 6.4); with host closures it cannot.}
}

\p{Conclusion for RLVM: do not compile Lean lambdas to host closures in the checker. If a Truffle engine is built, give it design E's job (section 1.6) and feed it definitions, not terms: a definition body is reused across calls, a proof term is evaluated once.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Environment representations}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 Options and measurements}

\table{
\tr{\th{Representation}\th{extend}\th{lookup i}\th{closure capture}\th{Evidence}}
\tr{\td{Linked list (cons per binding, shared tails)}\td{O(1), 1 node}\td{O(i)}\td{O(1): pointer}\td{Fastest in Guest0x0 (3.2). smalltt \code{\verb!!|Env = ENil | EDef Env ~Val!!} (\code{\verb~|smalltt/src/CoreTypes.hs:32-35~}), elaboration-zoo, Kovacs' interpreter, sokonanoda \code{\verb~|Cons~} nodes, gruszka}}
\tr{\td{Linked list + Myers jump pointer}\td{O(1), 1 node + 1 word}\td{O(log n)}\td{O(1)}\td{nanoclo \code{\verb~|EnvNode{entry,parent,len,next_level,jump}~} (\code{\verb~|nanoclo/src/closure.rs:36-44~}); jump rule at \code{\verb~|:416-426~}: \code{\verb~|jump = (len(p)-len(p.jump) == len(p.jump)-len(p.jump.jump)) ? p.jump.jump : p~}; lookup at \code{\verb~|:592-607~}}}
\tr{\td{Skew-binary random-access list}\td{O(1) amortised, up to 2 nodes}\td{O(log n)}\td{O(1)}\td{1.0-2.2x slower than list in Guest0x0}}
\tr{\td{Balanced map keyed by level}\td{O(log n) alloc}\td{O(log n)}\td{O(1)}\td{up to 7.7x slower}}
\tr{\td{Flat array, copy on extend}\td{O(n)}\td{O(1)}\td{O(1)}\td{not used by any arena checker for binder environments}}
\tr{\td{Flat closure (copy only captured variables)}\td{O(\verb~|#~free vars) at closure creation}\td{O(1)}\td{-}\td{ZINC/Coq VM \verb~|[~lit\verb~|]~; cadenza capture layouts; sokonanoda "Framed" pruned frames: 64-bit mask + contiguous slots, index by \code{\verb~|popcount(mask & ((1<<i)-1))~} (\code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/value.rs:858-888~})}}
\tr{\td{Frame chain (array per function activation + parent link)}\td{O(1) inside a frame}\td{O(depth of nesting)}\td{pointer}\td{natural for a compiled engine (design E), where a definition's \code{\verb~|n~} lambdas are entered at once}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 Lean-specific pressure}

\p{Lean terms have long lambda/let telescopes: lazylean reports a con-leche proof 73 binders deep and nanoclo a Mathlib subterm reached under 543 different binder telescopes (their READMEs); the arena has \code{\verb~|beta-ladder~} and \code{\verb~|let-ladder~} tests for exactly the O(n^2) behaviour of substitution on such spines. Yet all four closure-based leaders use linked lists (nanoclo adds jump pointers; it is the slowest of them at 8.4 min, cause not investigated). Recommendation: linked hash-consed list, specialise \code{\verb~|lookup~} for indices 0-2, add the jump pointer only if profiles show long walks.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Pruning by free-variable mask (the key trick)}

\p{\code{\verb~|prune(env, mask)~} returns the canonical environment that agrees with \code{\verb~|env~} on exactly the variables an expression reads. It is used (a) when building a closure or thunk, so garbage bindings are not retained and equal closures get equal ids, and (b) as the cache key for \code{\verb~|eval~} and \code{\verb~|infer~}. lazylean: "Measured on Std, pruning turned 41\% of evaluation misses into hits"; nanoclo's 543-telescope example is inferred once instead of 543 times.}

\pre{\verb!!|
prune(env, mask):
  if mask == 0: return base(lsub(env))
  if env.mask & ~mask == 0 and env.len <= highestBit(mask)+1: return env     // already pruned
  hit = pruneCache[hash(env, mask) & (2^14 - 1)]; if hit.key == (env, mask): return hit.val   // direct-mapped
  simple variant: k = highestBit(mask & ~FAR) + 1
     out = (mask & FAR) ? dropFirst(env, 63) : base(lsub(env))
     for i = k-1 downto 0: out = cons(out, bit(mask, i) ? lookup(env, i) : HOLE)
  compact variant (sokonanoda prune_env_cold, eval/environment.rs:123-214):
     one "frame" node = (mask, slots[popcount], cut, rest) interned by hash of its slots
  pruneCache[...] = ((env, mask), out); return out
!!}

\p{The 14-bit direct-mapped memo is sokonanoda's (\code{\verb~|checker/cache.rs:10-11~}, \code{\verb~|eval/environment.rs:75-89~}). Masks cover 63 indices; a term that reads further sets the "far" bit and keeps the tail unpruned. lazylean notes that letting an unknown mask propagate upward was a real performance bug (a 73-binder proof went from 31 M conversion steps to 200 k once exact masks were derived for terms whose own variables fit).}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.4 Levels for values, fresh variables, no weakening}

\ul{
\li{Terms use de Bruijn indices (stable under closure capture); values use de Bruijn \em{levels} (stable under context extension), so a value never needs shifting when it moves under a binder (all NbE sources).}
\li{A fresh variable is \code{\verb~|mkFVar(level = current depth, type)~} and is \strong{hash-consed by (level, type)} (\code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/eval/environment.rs:14~}, used at \code{\verb~|conv.rs:274, 289~}; lazylean README; nanoclo: "naming it by that level, rather than by a counter, makes two openings of the same binder produce the same variable"). Opening the same binder twice yields the same ids for everything built under it, so the eval, infer and conversion caches hit on re-entry. No global counter, no renaming.}
\li{Depth-independent alternative: gruszka gives a binder "the first variable of its type that is not in scope" and pestka names a variable "by its type, value and how many of its kind are open", so the same subterm opened at two different depths still shares caches.}
\li{The variable carries its type, so no typing context is needed to type a neutral (section 8.3).}
\li{\verb~|[~hyp\verb~|]~ Soundness of sharing such variables across unrelated binders: every cached fact (whnf, type, conversion) is a deterministic function of the value graph, and a variable's identity includes its type, whose own free variables are again (level, type) pairs; a fact about \code{\verb~|FVar(l, T)~} therefore holds for any variable of that type at that level. The arena checkers rely on this; RLVM must still fuzz it.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Call-by-need}

\table{
\tr{\th{System}\th{Policy \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~}}
\tr{\td{smalltt}\td{lazy: top-level and local definitions, Pi instantiation during elaboration, spines of top-level-headed applications; \strong{everything else call-by-value} (\code{\verb~|README.md:296-325~}; \code{\verb~|appCl'~} strict vs \code{\verb~|appCl~} lazy, \code{\verb~|Evaluation.hs:25-32~}; \code{\verb!!|Let _ _ t u -> let ~vt = ...!!}, \code{\verb~|:84~})}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{\strong{strict} arguments (\code{\verb~|eval.rs:238-256~}), definitions never unfolded by \code{\verb~|eval~}; iota fires in \code{\verb~|eval~} only when the major premise is already a constructor/literal (\code{\verb~|eval.rs:219-300~})}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{hash-consed \code{\verb~|Thunk{term, env, ls}~} "evaluated on demand and memoized"; an argument whose binder is never read is not evaluated; a casesOn reduces only its major premise}}
\tr{\td{lazylean / rpylean / gruszka ground machine}\td{call-by-need machine for closed computation: thunks overwritten on first force, environment dropped once the value is known}}
\tr{\td{Guest0x0 \code{\verb~|NBE.lazy~}}\td{lazy everywhere: 1.1-2.0x slower than strict when laziness saves nothing}}
\tr{\td{GHC HOAS CBN vs CBV}\td{0.63-1.24x: free only where the runtime has native thunks}}
}

\p{Rules for RLVM:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Thunk representation}: in design D a thunk is the hash-consed pair \code{\verb~|(expr, pruned env)~} whose WHNF slot receives the result. It costs one intern probe, the same probe a strict evaluator spends on its \code{\verb~|(env, expr)~} memo, so laziness is nearly free here, and two occurrences of the same delayed computation are one thunk (nanoclo: "\code{\verb~|f x~} reached under two environments that agree on \code{\verb~|f~} and \code{\verb~|x~} is one thunk"). In design E a thunk is an object \code{\verb~|{code, env} -> {value}~} updated in place, with \code{\verb~|env~} cleared on update (gruszka: "the environment goes once the value is known") and the captured environment trimmed to the variables the delayed term reads ("the classic space leak").}
\li{\strong{Do not thunk cheap arguments}: bound variables (already a value or thunk: pass the id), constants, literals, sorts, lambdas (closure creation is O(1) plus pruning). Thunk applications, projections of non-variables, lets, Pi types.}
\li{\strong{Do not evaluate unused arguments at all}: \code{\verb~|ignoresBinder(body)~} is one bit of the free-variable mask; sokonanoda substitutes a placeholder (\code{\verb~|infer.rs:293-297, 316-329~}), gruszka does the same before unfolding a definition whose body never reads a parameter.}
\li{\strong{Blackholing}: kernel terms have no recursive bindings, so a thunk can only depend on itself through a bug; gruszka's machine treats "a value that asks for itself" as a reason to give the run back. Keep a blackhole marker in debug builds. Per-worker arenas remove any need for atomic updates.}
\li{\strong{Selector thunks}: a delayed \code{\verb~|proj i t~} should, when forced, reduce to the field if \code{\verb~|t~} evaluates to a constructor application and otherwise become a \code{\verb~|PROJ~} spine cell; the result goes into the thunk's slot so the structure is not retained through the thunk.}
\li{\strong{Strictness that is safe for conversion checking}: because \code{\verb~|eval~} never performs delta (constants stay folded), eagerly evaluating an argument can only cost beta/zeta/projection work bounded by the syntax it traverses (and is shared through the caches). What must stay lazy is \em{unfolding} and \em{iota on unfolded majors}, which conversion drives. Strictness never changes an answer, only cost; the dangerous cases are closed computations (\code{\verb~|decide~}), which are precisely the ones routed to engine E.}
\li{\strong{In-place reuse / uniqueness bits} (the reussir idea): impossible for hash-consed values (shared and immutable by construction), natural in engine E, where gruszka's machine uses reference-counted objects, in-place thunk update and frees without recursion.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Conversion checking on values}

\subtree{
\title{8.1 smalltt: glued evaluation and rigid/flex/full \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~}

\ul{
\li{Values (\code{\verb~|smalltt/src/CoreTypes.hs:39-46~}): \code{\verb!!|VLocalVar Lvl Spine | VFlex MetaVar Spine | VUnfold UnfoldHead Spine ~Val | VLam NameIcit Closure | VPi NameIcit VTy Closure | VU | VIrrelevant!!}. A top-level constant evaluates to \code{\verb~|VUnfold head SId v~} and application acts on both components: \code{\verb~|VUnfold h sp v -> VUnfold h (SApp sp u i) (app ms v u i)~} (\code{\verb~|Evaluation.hs:34-40~}), the second being a lazy thunk.}
\li{Three unification states (\code{\verb~|Unification.hs:321-382~}, \code{\verb~|forceCS~} at \code{\verb~|:19-23~}): \strong{Rigid} (start): same unfold head -> compare spines in Flex; on failure unfold both and continue in \strong{Full}; different heads -> unfold both, stay Rigid. \strong{Flex}: no unfolding allowed; same head -> spines, anything needing an unfolding fails. \strong{Full}: always unfold first. Hence "at most one backtrack on any path to a sub-value"; the generalisation to N shots "did not show much difference" (\code{\verb~|README.md:383-390~}).}
\li{\code{\verb~|quote~} takes an unfolding option (\code{\verb~|UnfoldAll | UnfoldMetas | UnfoldNone~}, \code{\verb~|Evaluation.hs:162-180~}): the same value can be read back small (head + spine) or normal.}
\li{Paired values \code{\verb~|G {g1, g2}~} keep the least-reduced and the most-reduced form of a type so that neither forcing work nor small size is lost (\code{\verb~|README.md:400-417~}).}
\li{Rare cases (\code{\verb~|VIrrelevant~}, eta) are moved to a \code{\verb~|noinline~} cold function (\code{\verb~|:388-414~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.2 The Lean adaptation (sokonanoda, also lazylean, gruszka, nanoclo) \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~}

\p{Differences from smalltt that RLVM should copy:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{No thunk per application.} An unfoldable constant evaluates to a head node; applying it only extends the hash-consed spine (\code{\verb~|eval.rs:403-425~}). The unfolding is computed on demand by applying the constant's evaluated body to the whole spine at once and is memoised in the spine cell's STEP slot (\code{\verb~|eval/reduction.rs:373-441~}): "a constant unfolds at most once per instantiation". This removes the 2x glued-evaluation overhead smalltt reports.}
\li{\strong{Heights instead of "unfold both".} Different heads: unfold the side whose hint is greater (\code{\verb~|ReducibilityHint::is_lt~}, \code{\verb~|checker/env.rs:23-35~}: opaque < regular(h) < abbrev); equal -> both. The first attempt for each side is a non-forcing unfold; only if neither moves is a forcing unfold tried (\code{\verb~|conv.rs:330-365~}).}
\li{\strong{Budgeted same-head probe instead of all-or-nothing Flex.} For \code{\verb~|f as =?= f bs~}, each argument pair is compared with a budget of 2048 conversion steps (\code{\verb~|PROBE_CAP~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:134, 501-535~}); "exhausted" is a third outcome meaning \em{undecided}, never "false"; then proof irrelevance, then unfold both. gruszka: 256 ticks per pair, last argument first, and an outer race with doubling budgets (architecture.md "Conversion order").}
\li{\strong{Negative results under a budget go to a separate cache} (\code{\verb~|conv_cache_neg_probe~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:94-131~}) so that a truncated "false" is never reused as a definitive one.}
\li{\strong{Recursors and projections are heads too}: stuck \code{\verb~|rec~} applications are compared by spine after an iota attempt (\code{\verb~|unify_iota~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:551-589~}).}
\li{\strong{Nat literals}: comparison shortcuts for literal vs \code{\verb~|succ~}/\code{\verb~|zero~} forms (\code{\verb~|conv_nat~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:840-858~}); Nat binary operations are deferred while the other side can still unfold (gruszka "NatFoldWaits", sokonanoda \code{\verb~|nat_red_defer~}, \code{\verb~|eval.rs:621-635~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.3 Rules that need types, and how to get them without a context}

\table{
\tr{\th{Rule}\th{Needs}\th{How \verb~|[~src: \code{\verb~|sokonanoda/src/checker/conv.rs:690-838~}, \code{\verb~|eval.rs:493-577~}\verb~|]~}}
\tr{\td{function eta (\code{\verb~|\x. b~} vs neutral \code{\verb~|n~})}\td{nothing: compare \code{\verb~|b[x]~} with \code{\verb~|n x~} under a fresh variable; the variable's type is the lambda's domain}\td{\code{\verb~|unify_cold~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:695-712~}}}
\tr{\td{proof irrelevance}\td{type of each side, and that the type is a \code{\verb~|Prop~}}\td{only tried when both sides are neutral (or lambdas); \code{\verb~|value_type(v)~} walks the head's type through the spine, memoised in the value's TYPE slot; \code{\verb~|is_prop_type~} = sort of the type is level 0; then the two types are compared}}
\tr{\td{structure eta (\code{\verb~|x~} vs \code{\verb~|S.mk a b~})}\td{that the type whnf's to a structure \code{\verb~|S~} with one constructor}\td{\code{\verb~|try_struct_eta~}: types of both sides, compare \code{\verb~|proj_i(x)~} with field \code{\verb~|a_i~}}}
\tr{\td{unit-like types}\td{the type has one constructor with zero fields}\td{same function, returns true after the types agree}}
\tr{\td{skipping proof arguments in a spine}\td{which argument positions are proofs}\td{relevance signature per \code{\verb~|(constant, levels)~}: bitmasks \code{\verb~|prop_arg~}, \code{\verb~|absent_arg~}, \code{\verb~|prop_result~} computed once from the constant's type (\code{\verb~|checker/relevance.rs:13-52~}); a proof position is skipped once the preceding arguments agree; \code{\verb~|result_is_not_proof~} lets proof irrelevance be skipped without inferring a type}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|value_type~} requires \em{typed heads}: \code{\verb~|FVAR(level, type)~}, constants via the memoised \code{\verb~|const_head_type(name, levels)~}, literals by their inductive type. Lambdas and Pis are never asked for their type on this path (\code{\verb~|value_type_opt~}, \code{\verb~|conv.rs:742-747~}).}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.4 What can be memoised in an NbE world}

\table{
\tr{\th{Cache}\th{Key -> value}\th{Source}}
\tr{\td{closed-term evaluation}\td{\code{\verb~|expr~} (plus level-substitution id) -> value}\td{sokonanoda \code{\verb~|eval.rs:41-55~}}}
\tr{\td{open-term evaluation}\td{\code{\verb~|(prune(env, mask(expr)), expr)~} -> value, only for App/Proj/Let/Pi/Lambda nodes}\td{\code{\verb~|eval.rs:56-70~}; gruszka's thunk identity is the same key}}
\tr{\td{constant head, constant type, result sort}\td{\code{\verb~|(name, levels)~} -> value}\td{\code{\verb~|eval.rs:318-373~}}}
\tr{\td{per-value slots}\td{STEP (one unfolding / lambda domain), WHNF, TYPE}\td{\code{\verb~|value.rs:241-253~}; gruszka \code{\verb~|Memo{whnf, core, infer, uf}~}, \code{\verb~|tc.rs:601-618~}}}
\tr{\td{conversion}\td{ordered id pair -> true (always) / false (rigid, non-lambda, not truncated)}\td{\code{\verb~|conv.rs:94-131~}}}
\tr{\td{inference}\td{\code{\verb~|(pruned env, expr)~} -> type value + "checked under which universe parameters"}\td{\code{\verb~|infer.rs:164-170, 258-266~}}}
\tr{\td{quote}\td{\code{\verb~|(value, depth)~} -> term}\td{\code{\verb~|quote.rs:10-13~}}}
\tr{\td{relevance signatures}\td{\code{\verb~|(name, levels)~}}\td{\code{\verb~|relevance.rs:103-120~}}}
\tr{\td{persistent closed whnf store}\td{digest of a closed value -> quoted result, admitted on second sighting}\td{\code{\verb~|eval/whnf_cache.rs:22-69~}}}
\tr{\td{sessions}\td{the whole arena and all tables live across consecutive declarations}\td{gruszka "Sessions"; lazylean "Sessions"}}
}

\p{Sessions are valid because the environment only grows and a value means the same thing in later declarations; they must end when a declaration fails, when order goes backwards, and when a size budget is reached (gruszka lists the exact conditions).}
}

\subtree{
\title{8.5 Hash-consing of values: the two opposing positions}

\ul{
\li{Kovacs (\code{\verb~|smalltt/README.md:270-294~}): skip it; it does not compress beta-normal forms (\code{\verb~|oneMillion~} as a Peano numeral is incompressible), and it has overhead. True for an \em{elaborator} whose conversion problems are mostly small and fresh.}
\li{gruszka (architecture.md "Values"): "Without this sharing, two unfoldings of the same definition produce equal but separate graphs, and comparing them can take as long as running the definition. In Init, that was the difference between seconds and divergence." True for a \em{kernel}, whose input is a hash-consed DAG where the same types recur millions of times.}
\li{Guest0x0 quantifies the overhead when nothing is shared: 1.5-15x for full hash-consing of terms, about 1.1x for a single inlined memo slot.}
}

\p{For RLVM the kernel position applies; the overhead is contained by making hash-consing the allocation path itself (flat records, one probe) rather than a wrapper around objects.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Type inference in NbE style}

\p{Signature (sokonanoda \code{\verb~|infer_value~}, \code{\verb~|checker/infer.rs:94-268~}; gruszka "Inference"): \code{\verb~|infer(mode, depth, env, ctx, expr) -> type value~}, \code{\verb~|mode in {Check, InferOnly}~}, \code{\verb~|env~} = values of bound variables, \code{\verb~|ctx~} = their types (sokonanoda keeps a parallel list; gruszka reads the type off the environment entry).}

\pre{\verb~|
infer(mode, d, env, ctx, e):
  VAR i        -> ctx[i]
  SORT l       -> Sort(succ l)             CONST c ls -> constType(c, ls)      (memo per (c, ls))
  LIT          -> Nat / String
  cache: key = (prune(env, e.fvMask), e); a Check result answers InferOnly queries, not vice versa
  LAM x:A. b   -> dom = eval(env, A)
                  if mode == Check: inferSort(A); fv = mkFVar(d, dom); infer(Check, d+1, env+fv, ctx+dom, b)
                  return mkPiInfer(prune(env, e.fvMask), ctx, e) with STEP := dom   // codomain NOT computed
  PI x:A. B    -> l1 = inferSort(A); dom = eval(env, A); fv = mkFVar(d, dom)
                  l2 = inferSort(B) under (env+fv, ctx+dom); return Sort(imax l1 l2)
  LET x:A:=v;b -> if Check: inferSort(A); conv(eval A, infer v) or reject
                  infer(mode, d, env + arg(env, v), ctx + eval(env, A), b)          // no abstraction needed
  APP f a1..an -> fty = infer(f)
                  for each a_k: P = whnf(fty) must be a Pi (reject otherwise)
                       if Check: conv(P.dom, infer(a_k)) or reject
                       fty = instantiate(P, ignoresBinder(P.body) ? HOLE : arg(env, a_k))
  PROJ i s     -> whnf(infer s) must be `S params`; walk S.mk's type with params and proj_j(s), j < i
instantiate(P, v):  PI       -> eval(cons(P.env, v), P.body)
                    PI_INFER -> infer(InferOnly, d, cons(P.env, v), P.ctx + dom(P), P.lamExpr.body)
~}

\p{Tricks:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Infer closures} (the central one): the type of \code{\verb~|fun x => b~} is a Pi whose codomain is "infer \code{\verb~|b~} when instantiated" (\code{\verb~|Closure::mk_infer~}, \code{\verb~|infer.rs:190-215~}; \code{\verb~|apply_closure~}, \code{\verb~|eval.rs:452-474~}; gruszka \code{\verb~|Val::CloI~}, \code{\verb~|tc.rs:535-538~}; lazylean: "the type of a lambda is a Pi whose body is inferred when it is instantiated"). No quoting, no abstraction over the fresh variable, and in InferOnly mode the body is not even visited. Contrast smalltt, which must quote: \code{\verb~|valToClosure~} = \code{\verb~|Closure env (quote (lvl+1) UnfoldNone t)~} (\code{\verb~|smalltt/src/InCxt.hs:76-79~}, used at \code{\verb~|Elaboration.hs:177~}).}
\li{\strong{Telescope walking}: while the function type is a Pi whose codomain closure is a plain term, keep \code{\verb~|(tenv, piExpr)~} and step through nested Pi \em{syntax}, extending \code{\verb~|tenv~}, instead of building and forcing a Pi value per argument (\code{\verb~|infer_app_v~}, \code{\verb~|infer.rs:270-337~}). mathgraph adds "avoid forcing an inferred function type when it is already a Pi" (\code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/checkers/mathgraph.yaml~}).}
\li{\strong{Arguments are evaluated lazily or not at all}: the codomain is instantiated with the argument's thunk, or with a placeholder when the codomain ignores its binder (non-dependent arrows: the common case).}
\li{\strong{Two modes}: Check verifies argument types; InferOnly (used on already-checked terms, inside infer closures and for proof irrelevance) trusts them. Closed subterms checked once are recorded (\code{\verb~|checked_closed~}, \code{\verb~|infer.rs:172-186, 252-254~}) and gruszka shares them across workers stamped with the declaration that produced them.}
\li{\strong{Caching under binders} works because the key is the pruned environment: the fresh variable for a binder is determined by (level, type), so re-entering the same binder with the same relevant bindings reproduces the same key.}
\li{\strong{Top-level check}: \code{\verb~|conv(infer(Check, value), eval(declaredType))~} on values; nothing is quoted.}
}

\p{When quoting is unavoidable \verb~|[~src\verb~|]~: sokonanoda calls \code{\verb~|quote~} only from the inductive-type machinery (\code{\verb~|checker/inductive/{spec,recursor,restore,mod}.rs~}, building constructor/recursor telescopes as terms to compare with the export) and from the persistent closed-whnf store (\code{\verb~|eval/whnf_cache.rs:59-63~}). nanoclo: "substitution into a body happens only when a type leaves the checker". Keep \code{\verb~|quote~} memoised by \code{\verb~|(value, depth)~} and emit hash-consed terms.}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. Known pathologies and remedies}

\table{
\tr{\th{Pathology}\th{Evidence}\th{Remedy}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Exponential readback / loss of sharing}: the value graph is a DAG, naive \code{\verb~|quote~} and naive \code{\verb~|conv~} walk it as a tree}\td{Guest0x0 \code{\verb~|exponential~} and \code{\verb~|parigot_add~} (2-6 s vs microseconds with a memo); smalltt TreeConv}\td{never quote on the main path; identity test first; memoise quote by \code{\verb~|(value, depth)~}, conversion by id pair; emit hash-consed terms. Level-dependent output defeats a one-slot memo (v3: 8.6x vs named: 61000x): key closed values without the depth}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Sharing lost across unfoldings} (two unfoldings of one definition give two graphs)}\td{gruszka Init: "seconds vs divergence"}\td{hash-cons values, environments, spines; memoise the unfolding in the spine cell}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Glued-evaluation overhead} (a thunk per application of an unfoldable head)}\td{smalltt: 2x on ForceTree}\td{head + spine only; unfold on demand, memoised}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Optimistic same-head comparison that backtracks exponentially}}\td{smalltt README on unbounded optimism; gruszka trap \code{\verb~|s2bad~}: 21 G with a budget, 785 G with unbounded congruence}\td{budgeted probe with a third "undecided" outcome; separate cache for truncated negatives; at most one speculative attempt per path, or doubling budgets}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Reference order of lazy delta computes what neither side needs}}\td{gruszka "Conversion order": three CFSG proofs on which the official kernel exceeds 24-100 GB}\td{change only the \em{order} of sound steps: Nat folds wait, look for a common head first, compare a recursor-headed proof by its type before reducing it}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Eta loops / non-termination}}\td{arena \code{\verb~|eta-struct-major-*-no-whnf~} tests, on which the official kernel does not terminate (tenet-jvm README); gruszka cites lean4\verb~|#~15351 (structure eta before the major is reduced)}\td{function eta only when exactly one side is a lambda; structure eta only when one side is a constructor application and after the types agree; unit-like check compares types only; step budgets on speculative paths}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Deep recursion / stack overflow}}\td{Kovacs: every runtime except GHC needed stack tuning, Scala \code{\verb~|-Xss1000M~}, luajit could not run; tenet-jvm runs the checker in a large-stack thread; gruszka: 913 500-element list literal overflowed infer/defeq, million-step lazy accumulator declined at a 1 GiB stack}\td{see 10.1}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Memory proportional to work, not to live data}}\td{lazylean motivation (Lean's kernel at 166-178 GB); gruszka 286 GB before regions}\td{sessions with a size budget; gruszka regions (roll back the arena after a whnf whose result reaches <= 1/4 of what it built); route closed computations to engine E, where garbage dies}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Space leaks through environments}}\td{gruszka ground machine: \code{\verb~|f i~} kept the accumulator alive}\td{prune environments to the free-variable mask; clear a thunk's environment on update}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Compilation cost exceeding evaluation cost}}\td{Guest0x0: 0.1-16 s compile vs milliseconds of work; section 4: JIT is 13-65\% of CPU when cold}\td{interpret; compile only definitions that are provably hot (gruszka derives rules after 32 unfoldings; rpylean JITs only recursor back-edges)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{GC and JIT threads inflating the metric}}\td{section 4: 3.6-4.9x from the collector alone}\td{SerialGC, large nursery, non-traced value heap, pinned compiler threads}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Stale ids after an arena reset}}\td{gruszka \code{\verb~|SK_POISON~}, region gate}\td{generation stamps on direct-mapped caches; a poison mode in tests that shifts id spaces at every reset}}
}

\subtree{
\title{10.1 Stack-safe design for the JVM}

\p{Facts: the default Java thread stack is 1-2 MB (\code{\verb~|ThreadStackSize~}; \code{\verb~|ulimit -s~} does not govern threads created by the JVM) \verb~|[~lit\verb~|]~; a thread created with \code{\verb~|new Thread(group, runnable, name, stackSize)~} gets the requested size on HotSpot/Linux: the scratch benchmark of section 4 ran 1M-deep recursion on a 2 GB stack \verb~|[~measured-here\verb~|]~. \code{\verb~|StackOverflowError~} is catchable but leaves half-updated state unless every mutation is idempotent.}

\p{Layers, in order of preference:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Iterate over head steps.} \code{\verb~|whnf~} is a loop (beta, delta, iota, projection are tail steps); let-chains and application spines are peeled iteratively (\code{\verb~|eval.rs:186-195, 219-257~}); recursors waiting for their major premise go on an explicit stack (\code{\verb~|waiting: Vec<V>~}, \code{\verb~|eval/reduction.rs:162-216~}).}
\li{\strong{Run each worker on a thread with a large stack} (256 MB-1 GB): cheap, and the stack pages are only touched if used.}
\li{\strong{Depth counter with "hand-up"} for the remaining true recursion (nested forcing, infer and defeq on long cons-lists): when nesting passes a threshold (gruszka: 8192 nested whnf calls, or half the stack for infer/defeq), unwind with a preallocated control-flow exception carrying the value to work on; the outermost driver computes that value from a shallow stack (deepest first), stores it in its memo slot, and restarts the original call, which now hits the memo. Because results are memoised by id, the restart costs at most the threshold depth of repeated work (gruszka "Deep whnfs handed up", "Deep inferences and comparisons handed up").}
\li{\strong{Explicit continuation stack everywhere} (the abstract-machine style) only in engine E: Guest0x0 measures 1.0-3.5x overhead for heap continuations, so it is not the default for the checker.}
\li{Keep the mutator's stack shallow for the GC's sake as well (\verb~|[~hyp\verb~|]~, section 2.2).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. What the arena's closure-based checkers do (cross-reference)}

\table{
\tr{\th{Checker (Mathlib virtual time)}\th{Values}\th{Arguments}\th{Constants}\th{Environment}\th{Inference}\th{Extra}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean (65.8 s)}\td{NbE, hash-consing, de Bruijn; "beta reduction is just an environment push and every value remembers its own normal form" (\code{\verb~|nano-lean/README.md:9~})}\td{not read}\td{not read}\td{not read}\td{not read}\td{derived rewrite rules (cited by gruszka), PGO build, 4 threads}}
\tr{\td{gruszka (77.0 s)}\td{hash-consed \code{\verb~|Sort/Lit/Clo/CloI/Neu/Thunk~}, 32 B record incl. 4 memo words}\td{hash-consed thunks; unused ones never evaluated}\td{folded, unfolded by lazy delta; rules derived for hot recursive definitions}\td{hash-consed linked lists}\td{values, infer closures}\td{sessions (8 MiB), regions, ground machine past 2^26 values, 4 workers, PGO}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda (87.9 s), mathgraph (2.5 min)}\td{hash-consed 40 B nodes, spine cell = value}\td{strict}\td{folded (\code{\verb~|Unfold~} head), STEP memo}\td{cons + pruned frames, 64-bit masks}\td{values, infer closures, telescope walking}\td{relevance signatures, 2048-step probe}}
\tr{\td{lazylean (HEAD README: 0.48e12 instr, 1 core)}\td{hash-consed neutrals, envs, fresh vars}\td{-}\td{"constants stay folded ... the unfolding is stored in the value"}\td{pruned by 64-bit mask}\td{values, lambda type inferred on instantiation}\td{sessions (128 MB), Krivine machine with thunks for declarations that compute}}
\tr{\td{nanoclo (8.4 min)}\td{sokonanoda's values and conversion}\td{thunks named by the bindings they read}\td{-}\td{interned list + Myers jump pointers; substitution only at the boundary}\td{cache keyed by the bindings a term reads}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{rpylean (96.3 s)}\td{call-by-need CEK machine over persistent code nodes, RPython meta-tracing JIT, closed terms only with bail-out}\td{thunks}\td{-}\td{-}\td{(general \code{\verb~|machine.py~}, not studied here)}\td{JIT entered only on recursor back-edges}}
\tr{\td{kefir (7.2 min)}\td{"environment-based evaluator with call-by-need sharing; anything it cannot confirm is rechecked by a substitution-based checker" (yaml)}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm (27.2 min, Java)}\td{none: locally nameless substitution, lossy direct-mapped sharing table, ParallelGC, large-stack thread (\code{\verb~|tenet-jvm/docs/design.md~}, \code{\verb~|bin/tenet-jvm~})}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{the only JVM entry; 25x behind the NbE leaders}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. Verification status, risks, open questions}

\p{Not verified / not found:}

\ul{
\li{No Lean Zulip thread on "kernel vs NbE" was located by search; the closest primary source is \code{\verb~|smalltt/README.md:165-168~} ("Lean uses naive substitution in its kernel ... slower and more complicated than a straightforward NbE implementation").}
\li{"sixty" (Olle Fredriksson) is credited by \code{\verb~|elaboration-zoo/GluedEval.hs:5-7~} as the origin of glued evaluation; its README only lists glued evaluation as a roadmap item. No "staged NbE" write-up relevant to a kernel was found.}
\li{Agda's abstract machine (\code{\verb~|Agda.TypeChecking.Reduce.Fast~}: call-by-need environment machine with an STRef heap, compact case trees, memoised definition lookup) and Coq's VM / native_compute internals are quoted from documentation and papers, not from source.}
\li{Truffle engine options (compilation thresholds, compiler thread count) were not checked.}
\li{Section 4 measures CPU time, not instructions, on aarch64, one run per cell, on a toy workload. The collector and nursery effects are large enough to survive these caveats; the A/B/C ordering within 15\% is not.}
\li{nano-lean and rpylean's general checker were not read beyond their READMEs/headers.}
\li{Arena virtual times are the figures given in the project brief, not re-derived.}
}

\p{Open questions that need a spike before the core is frozen:}

\ol{
\li{Int-id arena vs Java objects for values on GraalVM (total CPU incl. GC, same algorithm).}
\li{Strict vs hash-consed-thunk arguments on a Mathlib sample (the \code{\verb~|arg~} switch of 1.3).}
\li{Simple HOLE-padded pruning vs sokonanoda's mask+slot frames.}
\li{(level, type) fresh variables vs gruszka's depth-independent naming: cache hit rates.}
\li{Whether design E is needed at all for the arena's test set, and if so whether a Truffle AST/Bytecode-DSL interpreter beats a plain Java machine once JIT threads are charged.}
}
}
